  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nFrost/freeze potential... Temperatures have dipped down into the\nlow to mid 30s within headline areas early this morning under \nmostly clear skies and light winds. With several hours yet for\ncooling to occur, current thinking is that headlines remain on \ntrack. However, with mid clouds knocking on our door, will have to\nsee whether or not temperatures end up steadying out. Otherwise,\nnext frost/freeze chances will be Sunday night and Monday night\nfollowing brief reprieve tonight. NBM showing 40 to 80% chance \nfor temperatures below 33 degrees mainly along the Upper Peninsula\nborder for both nights, with pretty slam dunk signal for below 36\ndegrees. As such, expect additional headlines to be issued for\nboth periods.\n\nRain chances... CAMs continue to trend slightly earlier with rain\nchances today through Sunday as WAA increases ahead of clipper-\nlike low to the north. Scattered showers are now progged to \narrive to central and north-central Wisconsin late this morning\nwith initial push of WAA, filling in later this afternoon and\nevening in proximity to approaching front. QPF amounts have\nlikewise increased, with probabilistic guidance now showing a 20 \nto 40% chance for receiving 0.5\" of QPF north of Hwy 29. \nRegardless, not expected to be drought busting for areas that \ndesperately need soaking rain. Both NAM-NEST and ARW try to\ngenerate lake effect showers along the Lake Michigan shoreline\nearly this morning, though remain doubtful that these will come to\nfruition due to lingering dry air. \n\nRidge of high pressure will continue to be the main driver of the\nsynoptic pattern through the extended, with most recent suite of \nensemble runs nearly wiping out precip chances during the mid- \nweek timeframe. Beyond that, not seeing any strong signals for\norganized systems through at least the middle of the month. With\nfrost/freeze headlines already in play, this may result in\nincreasing fire weather concerns over the next several weeks due\nto current drought status and increasing fine fuels.",
